<UL>
<LI>
<b>Background.</b> Non-fiction. Asimov. Period of sci-fi in the late 60s. Clarke
2001. Greatest Hits reader.
<LI>
<b>Oct 91</b> I was spending a great deal of time at work and started to worry
about my health. I started riding an exercize bicycle at night, and while
riding I would read. Switch between magazines and shortfiction.
<LI>
<b>Spring 92</b> First the first time since graduation, I had free time.
<LI>
<b>Oct 93 - Put the list of Hugo winners in a word processing document
with the intention of using it as a shopping list</b>. 
<p>
This began one Sunday
afternoon when the Chicago Bears were losing yet another football game, and I 
flipped over to the Sci-Fi Channel, where they were showing the director's 
cut of <i>Dune</i>. <i>Dune</i> isn't the greatest SF movie ever made, but 
for some inexplicable reason I can't stop watching it if it just happens to
come on the tube. At any rate, afterwards I felt a sudden urge to pick up the 
novel and read it again.
<p>
I had been side-tracked into "literature" and wasn't reading a lot of SF at
the time, but I thought that <i>Dune</i> stood up pretty well since my last 
reading. I knew that <i>Dune</i> was a Hugo winner, and feeling a little 
wanderlust at the time, thought that I might run into some more good SF 
if I tried reading other Hugo-winning novels (Like, I'm sure no one has ever
thought of this before).  I had most (volumes 1 thru 4) of Asimov's 
<i>The Hugo Winners</i> volumes already on hand, so I typed in the Hugo 
winning novels listed in those anthologies into a word processor.  Although 
I prefer short fiction, I didn't put those in the list because, obviously, I 
already had those stories on hand in the anthologies. So I started cruising 
the used book stores, buying and reading Hugo winners. 
<p>
At this point, I essentially had a database with 23 titles in it.
<p>
<LI>
<b>Feb 94 - Downloaded a list of Hugo and Nebula nominees and winners from
an ftp site, and converted the list into an awards database</b>.
<p>
After reading a few dozen short stories, as well as finding and reading
<i>The Demolished Man</i>, <i>Starship Troopers</i>, the revised
<i>Stranger in a Strange Land</i>, <i>The Man in the High Castle</i>,
<i>The Wanderer</i>, <i>The Moon is a Harsh Mistress</i>, <i>The Forever War</i>,
and <i>Gateway</i>, I ran into a problem: I couldn't find most of the other books
in local bookstores. Additionally, I had been away from SF for a long time;
<i>The Hugo Winner's,  Vol 4</i> didn't list any Hugo winners, and Volume 3
only covered winners up to 1975. I was missing almost 20 years of winners.
<p>
The Web was still a fledgling at this point; the explosion of new Internet
sites began in August 1994, WWW traffic first surpased FTP traffic in March 1995,
and the boom in WWW servers began about December of 1995.  Most WWW pages
on the Net at this time were from ftp archives, and there were no search engines.
There were, however, some gopher search engines, 
and it was pretty easy to find an ftp site that had both the Hugos <i>and</i> 
the Nebulas - winners <i>and</i> nominees. By this point, I was well aware
of the voting mechanism behind both awards, and knowing that only a couple of
points could separate a winner from a nominee, I thought that it would be
valuable to read not just the winners, but the nominees as well. I converted
the list into database format suitable for my UNIX system at work, as well as
my Windows system at home.
<p>
I now had an awards database with about 350 novel entries and 1000 short story 
entries.
<p>
<LI>
<b>Mar 94 - Downloaded the NESFA reading list, and converted it into a
searchable database.</b>
<p>
I found the NESFA reading list tucked away in some corner of a listing of
online resources.
<p>
<LI>
Apr 94    - Wrote a tool to find errors between the Hugo/Nebula list and
	    the NESFA reading list. Began feeding errors back to the owner
	    of the Hugo/Nebula list.
<p>

<LI>
<b>May 94 - Constructed a tool which created a reading list based on a point system.</b>
<p>

<LI>
Jun 94    - Fueled by the reading list, began acquiring additional awards.
<p>
<LI>
Sep 94    - Got a dump of David G. Grubbs awards files.
<p>
<LI>
Jan 95    - Combined the reading list and awards databases into a single database
	    that could be queried via the web.
<p>
<LI>
<b>Spring 95</b> - In 1995, the best SF portal (before they started calling them that) was
	<i>The SF Clearing House</i>, edited by John R. R. Leavitt. John was looking for 
	some sort of searchable awards database. I sent him a copy of my tool. Although it 
	would work, he was looking for something a little more "integrated". The Clearing
	House was trying to get magazine table of contents online (constructed manually), 
	and already had listings of awards, and he wanted to somehow link all these 
	together. Neither of us had any good ideas about how to do that at the time,
	because we were thinking in the reference of the SF Clearing House.
<p>
<LI>
<b>Summer 95</b> - I had come across the Internet Movie Database files in previous years,
        and in July found the webized version. This site blew me away for a number of
	reasons; aside from having useful content (which was previously available in a
	non-web version), I was impressed by two things:
	<p>
	<ol>
	<li> Results from a search displayed pages that were created by a machine.
	Since the pages were created when the user clicked on a link, it didn't
	require an army of error-prone people to write those pages (It still took
	an army of people to enter the data into the database, but that's a different
	problem). It also meant that disk storage (still at a premium) was only
	limited by the size of the database. It didn't matter if you could generate
	gigabytes of data in html pages - since they were created on demand, they didn't
	take up any space.
	<p>
	<li> The links didn't follow some predetermined topology. Most web pages at the
	time followed a strict hierarchy of a front page, which was like the root of a tree,
	which then branched of to leaf pages. Once you started searching in the IMDB,
	however, you could go in any number of directions (following genres, years,
	directors, actors, writers, awards, and so on).
	</ol>
	<p>
	At the same time I was cruising the IMDB, I also found a repository of John Wenn 
	bibliographies available via ftp. Someone else probably knows the history of
	the John Wenn bibliography format, but I got the impression that the layout was
	hammered out either in the SF newsgroups or in the SF Lovers newsletter. Most
	of the layout was pretty straightforward, although I particularly liked the
	way series were organized in a sort of outline format. 
	<p>
	Since I was playing with database tools, surfing the IMDB, and reading John
	Wenn bibliographies all at the same time, it wasn't much of a leap to go from 
	the IMDB to a version of an SF database that could output bibliographies in a 
	John Wenn-type format, that had links to awards, collaborators, publication data, 
	etc.  In fact, it only took a couple of hours to modify my current set of tools to
	output the information into the desired format. I took the output from
	one of these bibliographies and emailed it to Ahausrus to get his opinion. 
	He sent back about a dozen problems with it.  This started a lengthy cycle of
	modification/review over the course of the Summer, and into the Fall.
<p>
<LI>
<b>September 95</b> - ISFDB goes online.
<UL>

